\title{Twelve hexagons through every edge: one exterior edge}
\author{Human Compression project}\date{}
\begin{document}\maketitle

\begin{theorem}\label{main}
A finite simple quartic graph of girth six with exactly twelve hexagons
through every edge has at least thirty-five vertices.
\end{theorem}
This manuscript concerns the lower bound in the $\lambda=12$ row of
Table~2, physical page~18, of Goedgebeur--Jooken, \emph{Exhaustive
generation of edge-girth-regular graphs}, arXiv:2401.08271v2,
25 June 2024. It makes no existence assertion at order thirty-five.
The finite record specified below is part of the proof.

\section{The exterior edge determines both side profiles}
Fix an edge $uv$. Girth six gives two disjoint rooted trees of depth two,
excluding $uv$ at the first step. Together they have twenty-six vertices.
Each side has three branches, each with three leaves. The only possible
additional edges among these vertices join opposite leaves, and between
any two opposite branches they form a matching. These cross edges
correspond bijectively to hexagons through $uv$, so their number is
twelve. Writing their nine matching sizes as $3-d_{ij}$ gives
\[
  0\le d_{ij}\le3,\qquad \sum d_{ij}=15.                 \tag{1}
\]
There are fifteen missing leaf incidences on either side. If $Y$ is the
set of outside vertices and $t=|Y|$, the degree sum in $Y$ gives
\[
                         2e(Y)=4t-30.                    \tag{2}
\]
Thus $t\ge8$ and the graph has order $n\ge34$. Only order thirty-four must be excluded to prove the stated bound.

\begin{lemma}\label{edge}
At order thirty-four the eight vertices of $Y$ induce one edge $yz$.
The other six vertices have four leaf neighbours each, between one and
three on either side. The endpoints $y,z$ have three leaf neighbours
each; their six neighbours meet each of the six rooted branches exactly
once. In particular their combined degree on either side is three.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
Equation (2) gives $e(Y)=1$. Every other neighbour of a vertex of $Y$ is
a leaf. Two neighbours of the same outside vertex in one branch would
give a quadrangle, so at most three lie on either side. The six
nonendpoints therefore have positive side degrees complementary to four.
The endpoints cannot share a neighbour, since $yz$ would complete a
triangle. Nor can neighbours of different endpoints lie in the same
branch: the path through its parent, together with $yz$, is a pentagon.
Consequently the six endpoint neighbours lie in six distinct branches.
There are exactly six branches, proving the assertion.
\end{proof}

Let $P_L,P_R$ be the lists of missing leaf degrees, allowing zeros. Each
has sum fifteen. Put
\[
                       f(P)=\frac{45-\sum_{s\in P}s^2}{2}. \tag{3}
\]
There are twelve hexagons through $u$ avoiding $uv$: the four incident
edges count every hexagon through $u$ twice, and twelve use $uv$.
A right leaf of missing degree $s$ has $3-s$ cross neighbours, all in
distinct left branches. Its contribution to these other hexagons is
$\binom{3-s}{2}$. Subtracting the contributions of the nine old right
leaves leaves $f(P_R)$ pairs of left leaves supplied by outside vertices.
Similarly the outside contribution on the right is $f(P_L)$.

Write $a_1,\ldots,a_6$ for the left degrees of the nonendpoints, and
$b,c$ for those of $y,z$. Their right degrees are $4-a_i$, $3-b$, $3-c$.
Their sums on each side are fifteen. If $F_L,F_R$ are the corresponding
totals of pairs, then
\begin{align*}
F_L-F_R
 &=\sum_{i=1}^6(3a_i-6)+(2b-3)+(2c-3)\\
 &=3(12)-36+2(3)-6=0,
\end{align*}
where Lemma~\ref{edge} gives the last equality. Hence
\[
                    f(P_L)=f(P_R).                         \tag{4}
\]
The possible value is one of $7,8,9,10,11,12$. Indeed the six
nonendpoint degrees sum to twelve; enumerating them in $\{1,2,3\}$
and the two endpoint degrees in $\{0,1,2,3\}$ with sum three gives
$564$ vectors and exactly these six pair totals. These are necessary
conditions, not a classification of graphs by degree data.

We also retain the individual root equations. Let $L_0,L_1,L_2$ be the
left leaf branches and $a_i$ the number of cross edges meeting $L_i$.
For $\{i,j,k\}=\{0,1,2\}$ they are
\[
              \sum_w |N(w)\cap L_i|\,|N(w)\cap L_j|=a_k.     \tag{5}
\]
Indeed, if $b_{ij}$ counts hexagons through root edges $i,j$, then
$a_i+b_{ij}+b_{ik}=12$ and $a_0+a_1+a_2=12$, so $b_{ij}=a_k$.
Their simple paths correspond exactly to the common neighbours counted
on the left of (5). The same equations hold on the right. They remain
valid when $Y$ has an edge: that edge cannot occur in the four-edge path
between two branch parents defining such a hexagon.

\section{Covering all Moore configurations}
The constructor and verifier enumerate the integer matrices (1)
independently. They find $27\,876$ matrices and $458$ orbits under row and
column permutations and transpose, all actual symmetries of the rooted
trees. The acquired theorem on partial matchings along a forest supplies
the matching normalization. Its six vertices are the branch leaf sets.
In a forest block directed towards a new child, a permutation of the
child labels sends any matching to the increasing matching onto its
first $k$ labels; all parent $k$-subsets are retained. This permutation
acts also on the other blocks incident with that child. Induction away
from the forest roots proves coverage, and blocks outside the forest
retain all partial injective matchings. Reverse directions use the
transposed prescription. Unique representatives are not required.

The resulting inventory has $12\,864\,538$ normalized matching products.
Its traversal rejects newly formed quadrangles; the local graph is
bipartite. At a complete product it checks the six necessary equal
profile pairs above. Reuse of a root requires a literal
adjacency-preserving bijection fixing the root edge as an unordered set.
Nauty proposes the lookup key and bijection; neither is trusted without
this check. A false split only adds another root to process.
The complete traversal has $12\,026\,794$ prefixes and
$9\,484\,683$ girth-admissible products. Its $1\,560\,824$ literal
map checks retain $50\,542$ root graphs.

\section{Every remaining vertex and edge is explicit}
First partition the fifteen left incidences by their outside neighbour.
There are $b$ positive parts, where $6\le b\le8$, each of size at most
three. Only the two endpoints of $yz$ may have zero left degree. Insert
each incidence into every permissible current part or a new part,
naming new parts by first appearance. Repeated leaves, repeated branches,
an old common neighbour, or a repeated leaf pair give short cycles and
are rejected. Contributions to (5) may not exceed their targets; equality
is required at a completed partition. All these tests are necessary.

The repeated incidences of one leaf are inserted in increasing part-index
order. To see that this preserves coverage, take the partition of a
hypothetical graph, order its parts by their least leaf, and break ties
arbitrarily. Processing leaves in increasing order creates every new
part at its least leaf and visits already existing parts in increasing
order. It realizes the given partition up to outside relabelling.
Every right attachment for the retained labels is tried later.

There is a further necessary test on part sizes. If $F=f(P_R)$ and
$n_i$ counts the positive parts of size $i$, then
\[
 n_3=F-15+b,\quad n_2=45-2F-3b,\quad n_1=3b+F-30.           \tag{6}
\]
Retain nonnegative solutions. Append $8-b$ zeros, and try all choices
of two endpoint entries whose sum is three and which contain every zero.
Their complementary right sizes are $3-s$ at an endpoint and $4-s$
elsewhere; require these to lie between zero and three and have the
required right pair total. During a partial partition, an existing
part of size three must fit into a final part of size three, and a
part of size at least two into a final part of size at least two.
The current number of parts cannot exceed $b$, and the remaining
incidences must suffice to reach $b$. Reject only if none of the final
multisets in (6) accommodates these conditions.

For a completed partition, install its positive left parts as outside
vertices and append the empty parts. Try each unordered pair of distinct
outside labels as $yz$, requiring all empty parts to be endpoints and
the endpoint sizes to sum to three. Install that edge before any right
incidence, and reject a short cycle immediately. Vertex $j$ has residual
right capacity $3-|B_j|$ at an endpoint and $4-|B_j|$ elsewhere. These
capacities are nonnegative and sum to fifteen. Check their pair total
against (5).

Attach the fifteen right incidences to these eight fixed vertices,
trying every positive residual capacity. Repeated incidences of a right
leaf use increasing outside labels; they form an unordered neighbour
set, so this keeps one ordering of every possibility. Before adding
an edge require current distance at least five between its endpoints.
This is precisely the necessary condition for preserving girth six.
Track (5) on the right, rejecting only excesses or repeated branches
within one outside vertex. At termination require equality in all three
equations, zero residual capacities, degree four and girth six.
Finally count the length-five simple paths between each adjacent pair;
each such path together with that edge is a hexagon, so the count must
be twelve. Adjacencies, capacities and pair counts are restored on
return from every recursive call.

This parameterization is complete. Given a hypothetical graph, its left
neighbour partition occurs up to relabelling; label the possible empty
parts arbitrarily. Its actual exterior edge is one of those tried.
Lemma~\ref{edge} verifies every new restriction. Its right neighbour
sets are among the enumerated increasing sets and survive every monotone
test. When they have been attached, all thirty-four vertices and all
their edges are present. There is no unstated later completion.

\paragraph{Finite record.}
\input{completion_counts.tex}

The boundary initializer is checked separately against a program which
enumerates the degree vectors directly and constructs every tested graph
from its adjacency rows. All $784$ pair-total queries agree. On $15$
retained left partitions, all $420$ possible endpoint choices agree:
$58$ are accepted and $362$ rejected. This includes literal capacities
and all thirty-four adjacency rows. The actual right-incidence visitor
is also tested on all $512$ deletions of the nine right-to-outside edges
of the acquired order-thirty-two example with eighteen hexagons per
edge. It recovers one complete graph in each case. An independent
whole-neighbour-subset search, without the intermediate pruning, checks
$24\,136$ terminal candidates and gives the same answers. These controls
supplement the complete target record rather than replacing it.

\begin{proof}[Proof of Theorem~\ref{main}]
Equation (2) gives $n\ge34$.
At order thirty-four Lemma~\ref{edge} applies. The matrix and matching
traversals cover the Moore configuration of every hypothetical graph;
every reuse has a checked literal isomorphism. The complete boundary
parameterization then includes all its remaining vertices and edges.
The finite record excludes every such completion. This excludes order thirty-four and proves the bound.
\end{proof}

\paragraph{Attribution and scope.}
The lower bound is attributed to the fixed source row. The forest theorem,
literal-map method, root equations, ordered incidence partitions and
right-incidence visitor and the one-edge reduction are acquired from the
accepted fourteen-hexagon component and its stated predecessors. The present
argument treats eight exterior vertices, of which six have no outside
neighbour. This changes the inventory, side-size equations and complete
boundary calculation. The separately reviewed initializer cache preserves the original
accepted states by testing endpoint distance before inserting the sole
exterior edge. All target computations are separately recorded.
The same author wrote those acquired components. A separate agent gave
a preliminary check of the changed eight-vertex analytic counts; this is disclosed
separately from the final certificate review. There is no priority,
newly solved conjecture, whole-source, Lean or compression-factor claim.
